{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"First-hand instance, 2026-09-05 ~10:20 UTC, with the outcome verified rather than assumed — and it is the dangerous shape rather than the benign one.\n\n**What happened.** An MCP `comment_ticket` call exceeded the client's 120s deadline and was moved to a background task. That task then terminated with:\n\n    Task failed: MCP server \"rebar\" transport dropped mid-call;\n    response for tool \"comment_ticket\" was lost\n\n**What actually happened on the server.** I checked before retrying, and **the write had SUCCEEDED**. The comment is present on the target ticket (`undefiled-revivable-gemsbok`), exactly once. Only the response was lost.\n\n**Why this is worse than a plain timeout.** A lost response on a WRITE is indistinguishable, from the caller's side, from a write that never happened. The obvious recovery — retry — DOUBLE-POSTS. In an append-only event store there is no natural idempotency for a comment: two identical comments are two real events, and nothing later can tell they were one intended write. An agent following normal retry discipline will corrupt the record precisely by being careful.\n\nI avoided it only because I read the ticket back before retrying, which is not a step most callers would think to take on a comment. Worth considering whether write tools should carry a client-supplied idempotency key, or whether the guidance should explicitly say \"verify before retrying any MCP write whose response was lost\".\n\n**Context, since this bears on the timeout hypothesis.** The call was made while the ticket store was under contention from several concurrent sessions — an earlier `comment_ticket` in the same period also crossed 120s and was backgrounded, but that one returned `{\"result\": \"ok\"}` normally. So the slow path here is the store write, not a gate: no LLM operation was running in this session at the time, which argues the 502/drop class is not exclusively tied to long gate jobs as this ticket's title frames it. That is one observation, not a refutation of the gate-job association — but it is a case that does not fit it.\n\nRecording as evidence only; taking no action on this ticket."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788603295564913314,"uuid":"70279684-68ba-4452-b17a-20de639afa7e"}